26.6. F-(Variance-Ratio) Distribution Function
26.6.1   P(F|\nu_{1},\nu_{2})=\frac{\nu_{1}^{2}\nu_{2}^{3}}{B\Big{(}\frac{1}{2}\nu_{1}!\frac{1}{2}\nu_{2}\Big{)}}\int_0^\nu t^{(\nu_{1}-2)}(\nu_{2}+\nu_{1}t)^{-1(\nu_{1}+\nu_{2})}dt\]
26.6.2   Q(F|\nu_{1},\nu_{2})=1-P(F|\nu_{1},\nu_{2})=I_{\infty}\Big{(}\frac{\nu_{2}}{2},\frac{\nu_{1}}{2}\Big{)}\] where x=\frac{\nu_{2}}{\nu_{2}+\nu_{1}}F\] Relation to the Chi-Square Distribution
If X_1^1 and X_2^2 are independent random variables following chi-square distributions 26.4.1 with n_1 and n_2 degrees of freedom respectively, then the distribution of F=\frac{X_1^1/\nu_1}{X_2^2/\nu_2} is said to follow the variance ratio or F-distribution with n_1 and n_2 degrees of freedom. The corresponding distribution function is P(F|\nu_{1},\nu_{2}).
